                </ul>

                <h2 id="titles">Professional Overview</h2>

                <p>Throughout my <b>9-year career</b> across studios from indie to AAA, I have consistently occupied a central, cross-disciplinary role – owning features from the game loop on the device through to the tools, services, and data behind it. The majority of my work has been in <b>Unity</b>, shipping on PC, Android, iOS, PlayStation, Xbox, and Nintendo Switch, alongside professional <b>C++</b> engine and tool work on <b>Star Citizen</b>.</p>

                <p>My technical contributions span <b><a href="@explicitBaseUri/portfolio/tools" class="textual-link" target="_blank">authoring tools<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a></b>, <b><a href="@explicitBaseUri/portfolio/systems" class="textual-link" target="_blank">systems<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a></b>, <b><a href="@explicitBaseUri/portfolio/backend" class="textual-link" target="_blank">backend infrastructure<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a></b>, <b><a href="@explicitBaseUri/portfolio/multiplayer" class="textual-link" target="_blank">multiplayer<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a></b>, <b><a href="@explicitBaseUri/portfolio/gameplay" class="textual-link" target="_blank">gameplay<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a></b>, and <b><a href="@explicitBaseUri/portfolio/technicalart" class="textual-link" target="_blank">technical art<SvgSprite Link="/icons.svg?v=1#icon-external-link" class="external-link-icon" /></a></b>. This breadth lets me follow a problem from what a player feels down to the very system responsible for it, author fixes for it at the right layer, and collaborate credibly with every discipline on the team.</p>

                <h3>Tools, Workflows & UX</h3>

                <p>As a Tools Programmer, I think of myself as a <b>"Workflow Engineer"</b>, responsible not just for the technical delivery but also for the full experience of the people using what I build. I gather requirements directly from artists, designers, and producers, design solutions alongside them, and iterate against their feedback, balancing user impact, technical feasibility, and business goals. A tool no one knows how to use is a tool that fails, so I produce tutorial videos and documentation for end users alongside technical docs for engineers. Often the best solution is helping a team get more from what Unity already offers – a call I can make having used Unity since <b>Unity 4.1.2</b> in <b>2013</b>.</p>

                <h3>Live Service & Backend</h3>

                <p>I have extensive experience operating on live service games. On <b>LEGO® Star Wars™ Battles</b>, I built the server systems behind weekly leaderboards, clans, username moderation, and App Store and Google Play receipt validation, alongside the update delivery that let players on different game versions share the same servers, with telemetry integrated throughout. On <b>Twilight Towers</b>, I rewrote the player profile migration pipeline while the live game depended on it: I made the simulation run headless for unit testing, built a validation suite with soft-lock detectors, ran it against thousands of real player profiles, and replaced the legacy code with a data-driven system that made hotfix migrations safe to release.</p>

                <h3>Leadership & Code Quality</h3>

                <p>I have reviewed code and mentored developers throughout my career, from mentoring on <b>LEGO® Star Wars™ Battles</b> to working as a Staff engineer at <b>Fortis Games</b>. I'm disciplined in designing an API <i>before</i> the implementation, writing automated tests inside and outside the game engine that run in CI, and running handover sessions and preparing example pull requests so others can confidently own what I build. I have also shared my experience publicly, as a guest speaker at the <b>Brighton Unity User Group</b> in 2019 and a panellist at <b>EGX 2023</b>.</p>

